  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "caption": "Summary",
      "html": "<p>Once the genitive singular and the gender are known, most third-declension nouns are easy to recognize. χάρις, χάριτος has the accusative χάριν. Nouns in -ις, -εως (πόλις, πίστις) are feminine; nouns in -ος, -ους (γένος, ἔθνος, ὄρος) are neuter; nouns in -ευς, -εως (βασιλεύς, ἱερεύς, γραμματεύς) are masculine. In the last two classes a stem σ or υ has dropped out and the vowels have contracted. πατήρ, μήτηρ and ἀνήρ lose the ε of the stem in some cases (πατρός, μητρός, ἀνδρός). ἀληθής, ἀληθές is the first adjective declined by the third declension in all three genders.</p>"
    },
